{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Context / Problem\n`scripts/check_comment_hygiene.py` is wired into CI as its own step (`.github/workflows/_build-and-test.yml`, \"comment-hygiene gate\") and is covered locally only by `tests/unit/test_comment_hygiene_guard.py::test_the_real_tree_is_clean`, which runs in the **full** suite. It is **not** in `make lint`, so the local fast gate returns a clean verdict over trees CI rejects. Measured cost: bug 84c7 (change 1999) took a `Verified -1` for comment-hygiene findings `make lint` could not surface, wasting a full review cycle (two red jobs, the pytest one 15m09s with the hygiene guard as its only failure).\n\n## Approach\nEvidence gathered and recorded as a comment on this ticket resolves the ticket's option fork in favor of wiring the gate into `make lint`:\n- The introducing ticket `b047-267f-3c3d-4374` shows **no deliberate intent** to keep the gate out of `make lint` — its plan wired the detector as a named CI step per the `scripts/check_*.py` guard convention and never discusses the local lint target.\n- Measured runtime: ~4.9s warm on the full tree locally (`make lint` baseline 17.8s → ~22.7s). Acceptable next to the 15-minute CI cycle a miss costs, and squarely inside the documented env-var-drift-gate rationale already in the Makefile (\"running it here makes the local verdict agree with CI instead of surfacing staleness only in the full suite\").\n\nSo: add `python scripts/check_comment_hygiene.py` to the `lint` target next to `scripts/gen_env_registry.py --check`, mirroring that gate's precedent exactly — the standalone CI step in `_build-and-test.yml` **stays** (its if-present guard correctly handles patchsets predating the gate; the small double-run in CI matches the env-registry precedent). Add a wiring test in `tests/unit/test_comment_hygiene_guard.py` (the gate's own test module) asserting the `lint` target body invokes the script, using the `_lint_target_body` idiom from `tests/unit/test_config_gate_wiring_heldout.py`, plus a teeth case proving the assertion flips when the invocation is stripped from a synthetic Makefile copy.\n\n## Scope\n- `Makefile` — add the gate invocation (with a short rationale comment citing this ticket and the measured runtime) to the `lint` target.\n- `tests/unit/test_comment_hygiene_guard.py` — baseline wiring assertion + removal-teeth test.\n\n## Testing\nRED first: the new wiring test fails against the unedited Makefile. GREEN: it passes once the lint-target line lands. Edge/teeth: stripping the invocation from a synthetic Makefile copy flips the assertion (proves it is not vacuous). `make lint` (now including the gate), `make typecheck`, and the targeted test module all pass.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- [x] The introducing ticket `b047-267f-3c3d-4374` is checked for deliberate intent to keep the gate out of `make lint`, and the finding (none found) plus the measured runtime (~4.9s standalone; lint 17.8s → ~22.7s) are recorded as a comment on this ticket.\n- [x] `make lint` runs `python scripts/check_comment_hygiene.py` in the `lint` target — verified by running `make lint` and by the new wiring test in `tests/unit/test_comment_hygiene_guard.py`.\n- [x] The wiring test has teeth: a synthetic Makefile with the invocation stripped fails the same assertion, and this is itself a passing test at HEAD.\n- [x] `make lint`, `make typecheck`, and `pytest tests/unit/test_comment_hygiene_guard.py` pass locally."}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1787354963373951001,"uuid":"4b388d01-2f17-4b55-b95a-79b08ea16f5c"}